00:06:07.716 --> 00:06:11.796
and a 2014 decision analysis paper sets out two reasons for that shove.

00:06:12.396 --> 00:06:15.996
People usually blend many personal probabilities with a weighted average.

00:06:16.536 --> 00:06:20.516
The authors say that blend tracks events more closely once it is moved outward,

00:06:21.096 --> 00:06:22.076
closer to an endpoint.

00:06:22.756 --> 00:06:24.476
Reason one is the shape of the scale.

00:06:25.036 --> 00:06:30.636
It is pinched at both ends, so random misses cannot fall past zero or past one, and instead

00:06:30.636 --> 00:06:32.036
pile up on the inner side.

00:06:32.636 --> 00:06:34.596
The average gets dragged toward one half.

00:06:35.236 --> 00:06:36.836
A median does not take that drag.

00:06:37.376 --> 00:06:42.576
The mean of the law gods, they say, arguably escapes it too, because law gods stretch the

00:06:42.576 --> 00:06:43.256
pinched ends.

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:47.256
Reason two hits the mean, the median, and that law gods mean.

00:06:47.256 --> 00:06:52.396
Each person knows they have not seen every relevant fact, so they soften how hard they

00:06:52.396 --> 00:06:53.456
lean high or low.

00:06:54.036 --> 00:06:57.276
That private caution never counts evidence sitting with other people.

00:06:57.856 --> 00:07:01.876
The paper calls the gain from pooling those different scraps the wisdom of crowds.

00:07:02.576 --> 00:07:07.216
The group may already hold what each member was missing, yet the blend still carries every

00:07:07.216 --> 00:07:08.436
member's inward shade.

00:07:09.056 --> 00:07:13.876
One function they find can roughly cancel both problems, with one setting for the mean

00:07:13.876 --> 00:07:15.216
and another for the median.

00:07:15.216 --> 00:07:18.296
switching to the median helps tell the problems apart.

00:07:18.756 --> 00:07:23.036
Compression is an average problem and the ignorance problem is still there in the median.

00:07:23.636 --> 00:07:28.276
The abstract never prints those settings, so the size of the shove is not a number we have.

00:07:28.916 --> 00:07:30.976
Follow that chain without inventing figures.

00:07:31.636 --> 00:07:35.116
Error bunched inside the ends pulls a weighted average toward one half.

00:07:35.776 --> 00:07:38.376
Take the median and that particular pole drops away.

00:07:38.936 --> 00:07:42.636
Both numbers still embed each person's discount for facts they never held.

00:07:42.636 --> 00:07:48.596
then one transform pushes the blend toward an endpoint, harder or softer depending on whether you

00:07:48.596 --> 00:07:53.896
started from the mean or the median. The means extra push is their way of isolating compression.

00:07:54.476 --> 00:07:59.416
Whatever push the median still needs is the ignorance effect. They call the repair approximate,

00:07:59.696 --> 00:08:05.516
not exact. The abstract name is no outside critic. It hedges the law gods escape with the word

00:08:05.516 --> 00:08:11.256
arguably, and it hedges the cure as approximate. The second reason applies only when different people

00:08:11.256 --> 00:08:15.616
hold different evidence. A 2019 decision analysis review of combined

00:08:15.616 --> 00:08:21.876
probability forecasts discusses miscalibration, dependence among forecasters, and which

00:08:21.876 --> 00:08:27.196
score you use to judge them, and it ties aggregation to decisions. It does not run a test

00:08:27.196 --> 00:08:33.256
of the 2014 transform, so it neither backs the settings nor knocks them down. If the forecasts

00:08:33.256 --> 00:08:38.356
are copies of one shared error, the premise that the pool holds different evidence is not met.

00:08:38.356 --> 00:08:41.776
A contract price is not the object that paper repairs.

00:08:42.216 --> 00:08:46.156
It is not a weighted mean of separate forecasts, and it is not their median.

